+      <section id="limitations" class="section-card">
+        <h2>Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
+        <h3>Data limitations (must-read)</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> not all countries report each year. Global means/medians reflect only the
+            reporting subset.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Coverage variability:</strong> the number of reporting countries changes materially across time, which
+            can affect apparent trends.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Territories and special cases:</strong> some territories appear separately. UK data may refer to
+            England and Wales only, per dataset documentation.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>No regional mapping:</strong> region-level aggregation is not directly supported without external
+            mappings (not used here).
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Age-group coverage:</strong> age-specific data are sparse and available only for sex-disaggregated
+            series in this file.
+          </li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h3>Actionable recommendations (derived from observed patterns)</h3>
+        <ol class="small">
+          <li>
+            <strong>Target high-rate outliers with focused prevention packages:</strong> use the latest-year top tail to
+            prioritize technical assistance, violence interruption, firearm risk reduction, and organized-crime disruption
+            strategies.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Make gender disparity a dashboard KPI:</strong> track both the male-to-female ratio and sex-specific
+            levels; deploy interventions that address the dominant sources of male victimization while maintaining
+            protection services relevant for women.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Invest in statistical capacity and reporting continuity:</strong> improve coverage and consistency to
+            make global monitoring and evaluation more reliable.
+          </li>
+          <li>
+            <strong>Use multiple summary statistics in decision briefs:</strong> report both median and mean to avoid
+            decisions driven by extreme outliers or by overly “typical” values.
+          </li>
+        </ol>
